# The determinant, the scalar that measures how a linear map scales volume, and its central law: the determinant of a
# product is the product of the determinants. For two-by-two matrices this is a polynomial identity in the eight
# entries, proved for ALL values by the commutative-ring normalizer and checked by `seed test`. This multiplicativity
# is why the determinant descends to a homomorphism from the general linear group to the nonzero scalars, and why the
# special linear group (determinant one) is a subgroup. The rotations of the eight-dimensional arena live in this
# determinant-one world.

# The determinant of a product of two-by-two matrices equals the product of their determinants. With
# A = [[a, b], [c, d]] and B = [[e, f], [g, h]], their product A B has determinant (a e + b g)(c f + d h) minus
# (a f + b h)(c e + d g), and this equals (a d - b c)(e h - f g), the product of the two determinants. A universal law
# over all eight entries.
task determinant-is-multiplicative
  take a, like integer
  take b, like integer
  take c, like integer
  take d, like integer
  take e, like integer
  take f, like integer
  take g, like integer
  take h, like integer
  like integer
  hold <the determinant of a product is the product of the determinants>
    call is-equal
      call subtract
        call multiply
          call add
            call multiply
              read a
              read e
            call multiply
              read b
              read g
          call add
            call multiply
              read c
              read f
            call multiply
              read d
              read h
        call multiply
          call add
            call multiply
              read a
              read f
            call multiply
              read b
              read h
          call add
            call multiply
              read c
              read e
            call multiply
              read d
              read g
      call multiply
        call subtract
          call multiply
            read a
            read d
          call multiply
            read b
            read c
        call subtract
          call multiply
            read e
            read h
          call multiply
            read f
            read g
  send back
    read a
